# Are there more obese or more underweight adults in the world?
subtitle: "Two head-to-head rounds: too thin or obese, which is more common among the world's adults? Then see the year the two lines crossed."
tags: ["health","data"]
format: quiz
thumb: "band \"Too thin or too heavy?\" note \"The lines crossed\""
voices: {"a":"gemini:Algenib | a gravelly, twinkly quiz host with stories to tell; warm, natural pace","take":"lines"}

## Are there more obese or more underweight adults in the world?
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - 1975
      - 1980
      - 1985
      - 1990
      - 1995
      - 2000
      - 2005
      - 2007
      - 2010
      - 2014
    series:
      - {name: Underweight, values: [14.2, 13.7, 13.2, 12.8, 12.2, 11.6, 10.8, 10.4, 9.8, 9.2]}
      - {name: Obese, values: [null, 5, 5.5, 6.1, 7.1, 8.3, 9.8, 10.4, 11.3, 12.6]}
    xlim:
      - 1975
      - 2026
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 18
    y_label: '% of adults'
    label_size: 30
sources:
    - id: owid_ob
      title: Share of adults who are obese
      authors: Our World in Data, from the WHO Global Health Observatory
      year: 2025
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/share-of-adults-defined-as-obese
      finding: 'world: 5.0% of adults obese (BMI 30+) in 1980, 6.1% in 1990, 12.6% in 2014, 16.4% in 2024'
    - id: owid_uw
      title: Share of men / women defined as underweight, healthy, overweight and obese
      authors: Our World in Data, from NCD Risk Factor Collaboration (2017)
      year: 2017
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/men-weight-categories
      finding: 'world: underweight (BMI under 18.5) about 13.7% of adults in 1980 and 9.2% in 2014 (average of men and women)'
    - id: owid_cont
      title: Obesity rates have increased on every continent
      authors: Our World in Data
      year: 2024
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/data-insights/obesity-rates-have-increased-on-every-continent
      finding: adult obesity rates have risen on every continent
    - id: ncd
      title: Trends in adult body-mass index in 200 countries from 1975 to 2014
      authors: NCD Risk Factor Collaboration, The Lancet
      year: 2016
      finding: the world went from more underweight than obese adults in 1975 to more obese than underweight adults

    cards duel cites ["owid_ob","owid_uw"] size 2 unit % compare "Which is more common?" hidden true
        * "1980: too thin" value 13.7
        * "1980: obese" value 5
        * "2014: obese" value 12.6
        * "2014: too thin" value 9.2
    dot cross_pt #3d3833 at.data [2007,10.4] hidden true
    text cross_l "2007" #3d3833 at.data [2007,6.6] cites ["owid_ob","owid_uw"] font_size 52 hidden true
    text one7 "1 in 7" #b5482e at.data [1982,15.8] cites ["owid_uw"] font_size 48 hidden true
    text one20 "1 in 20" #2f6b8f at.data [1983,2.6] cites ["owid_ob"] font_size 48 hidden true
    text one6 "2024: 1 in 6" #2f6b8f at.data [2012,17.6] cites ["owid_ob"] font_size 52 hidden true
    dot p24 #2f6b8f at.data [2024,16.4] hidden true
    path p24_link dashed thick #2f6b8f points [[2014,12.6],[2024,16.4]] data true hidden true

    card title "More obese or underweight adults?"

Which is more common among the world's adults: being too thin, or having obesity? Two rounds, thirty-four years apart.
    draw duel

    ask "Tap the bigger share in each pair." on duel store h
        right: Both right.
        wrong: Not both. The answer flips between the two years.

    erase duel

Now the full story, year by year. In 1980, about one adult in seven was underweight.
    draw axes line_1 one7

Obesity was rarer, about one in twenty. But it climbed, year after year.
    draw line_2 one20

    erase one7 one20

Around 2007, the two lines crossed. Since then, obesity is the more common of the two.
    draw cross_pt cross_l

Part of that is good news: fewer adults are too thin, because fewer go hungry.
    highlight line_1

And obesity kept climbing: nearly tripled since 1990, to about one adult in six by 2024.
    draw p24_link p24 one6

So the world's weight problem has flipped: from too little food, to too much.
    text flip "too thin → too heavy" #3d3833 at.data [1996,2.6] font_size 48

